# Cross-Repository Accessibility Mapping:
# Production Management and Closed Caption Subtitles

## Purpose

This document maps the operational and production-management requirements in the Accessible Film, TV and Video Content Production Management repository to the editorial, technical and governance requirements in the Closed Caption Subtitles repository.

It provides a practical bridge between:
- production governance, responsibilities and release gates
- caption and subtitle authoring requirements
- evidence-based QA and publication checks

## Scope

This mapping applies to:
- video and audiovisual production
- caption and subtitle creation
- live and prerecorded content
- multiple versions, territories, languages and platforms
- AI-assisted production workflows

## Repository relationship

### 1. Production-management repository
The production-management repository defines the operational model for accessible media production. It focuses on:
- commissioning
- responsibility assignment
- briefing
- version control and asset provenance
- access-service planning
- QA evidence
- escalation and release gates
- archive and publication verification

### 2. Closed-caption repository
The Closed Caption Subtitles repository defines the detailed technical and editorial requirements for captions. It focuses on:
- speech and meaningful sound coverage
- timing and synchronisation
- speaker identification
- line breaks and presentation
- live captioning policy
- player accessibility controls
- file format and delivery profile selection
- AI governance and human review obligations

## Core mapping

### A. Commissioning and audience definition
| Production-management concept | Closed-caption equivalent | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| audience and territory matrix | CCS audience and platform profile | The production repo defines who the content is for and what access service is required; the caption repo defines the exact delivery profile for the caption track |
| commissioner brief and output requirements | CCS required content and language profile | Caption scope depends on audience, platform, language and delivery profile |
| access-service obligations and service targets | CCS as a required access service | CCS is one of the access services identified in production planning |

### B. Production responsibility and workflow
| Production-management concept | Closed-caption equivalent | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| accountable production owner | accessibility lead / QA owner | The production repo requires clear accountability; the caption repo assigns editorial and QA roles |
| pre-production briefing | caption author and editor briefing | The caption track must be informed by scripts, names, glossary, timing context and final media |
| production asset and version control | exact media version for captioning | CCS must be tied to the approved media version; version mismatch is not acceptable |
| review and acceptance flow | editorial QA and technical validation | Production governance requires evidence; CCS guidance defines what acceptance looks like |

### C. Capture and preparation
| Production-management concept | Closed-caption equivalent | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| clean dialogue and sound assets | CCS coverage of meaningful audio | The caption repo requires accurate speech and meaningful sound coverage |
| script, glossary and names | CCS review of names, terms and numbers | The caption repo requires checks against authoritative sources |
| visual and graphics inventory | CCS protection of essential picture information | Assets must be checked to avoid captions obscuring key information |

### D. Editorial and technical requirements
| Production-management concept | Closed-caption equivalent | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| access-service review gate | CCS 001–033 editorial and technical requirements | The production repo ensures the work is reviewed at staged gates; the caption repo specifies the required quality |
| timing and synchronisation | CCS 011–015 | The sync and reading-speed requirements are operational requirements that must be validated in the final media context |
| speaker identification | CCS 016–018 | Production governance requires this to be tested and recorded; caption guidance defines the actual methods |
| meaningful sound, music and lyrics | CCS 019–023 | Production planning must provide the required audio context for caption review |
| readable presentation and contrast | CCS 024–027 | The production repo requires presentation checks before release |
| live caption handling | CCS 028–030 | Live content requires the operational planning described in the production repo plus the live caption quality controls in the CCS repo |
| format and delivery profile | CCS 031–033 | Production delivery requires a documented profile; the CCS repo defines valid format choices and validation requirements |
| player control accessibility | CCS 13 | The production repo requires end-to-end publication verification; the caption repo specifies media-player requirements |

### E. Quality assurance and release gates
| Production-management concept | Closed-caption equivalent | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| gate-based verification | CCS section 14 | The production repo defines evidence-based gates; the caption repo provides the specific tests |
| specialist QA and audience testing | CCS acceptance tests and audience outcome testing | Production management requires review and, where proportionate, disabled audience evaluation |
| publication verification | CCS player and package validation | Captions are not considered delivered until the correct track is discoverable, labelled, and working in the target player |
| release decision | CCS minimum acceptance criteria | The release gate is only valid when the caption asset passes the relevant acceptance rules |

### F. AI-assisted production governance
| Production-management concept | Closed-caption equivalent | Relationship |
| --- | --- | --- |
| human briefing and review | CCS section 15 | Both repositories require human review and traceable accountability for AI-assisted workflow steps |
| evidence and audit trail | CCS section 15 machine-readable decision record | The production repo requires audit logs, records and escalation references; the CCS repo requires a review record |
| prohibited AI behaviour | CCS section 15 prohibited AI behaviour | The production repo requires governance controls and accountable decision-making; the caption repo defines what AI systems must not do |
| exception handling | CCS section 16 | Both repositories require explicit exception records, including affected requirement, audience impact, owner and corrective action |

## Operational mapping by lifecycle stage

### 1. Commissioning
- Production repo: define audience, territory, platform, access obligations, budget and owner
- CCS repo: define required caption track, language, delivery profile and requirements
- Outcome: a production brief that includes captioning obligations before asset creation

### 2. Pre-production
- Production repo: identify dependencies, script issues, names, technology requirements and creative risks
- CCS repo: check final audio, terminology, sound effects, speaker identification and timing risks
- Outcome: a production brief and captioning specification aligned to the final media

### 3. Production and post-production
- Production repo: manage versioned assets, access-service briefs and reviewer sign-off
- CCS repo: author, time and edit captions against final media
- Outcome: a finished caption file validated against the media and target delivery profile

### 4. QA and acceptance
- Production repo: require evidence-based gate review
- CCS repo: test editorial completeness, timing, file conformance, player operation, and audience outcome
- Outcome: a caption track that is accepted under the caption guidance and the production governance model

### 5. Publication and release
- Production repo: require publication verification, open issues tracking and release authority
- CCS repo: ensure the media player exposes the correct caption controls, the file is correctly labelled and the track remains functional after distribution
- Outcome: a published access experience that works for the intended audience

### 6. Archive and redelivery
- Production repo: maintain asset provenance, access records and archive evidence
- CCS repo: maintain format-specific delivery metadata and verification history
- Outcome: a full audit trail from master to final published asset

## Required controls when combining the repositories
The combined framework should ensure:

1. No caption track is treated as complete until the production management evidence is in place.
2. No production access decision is valid without the corresponding caption quality evidence.
3. All versions, languages and territories require a matching caption profile.
4. Human review remains mandatory where meaning, editorial judgement or final publication decisions are involved.
5. AI-assisted caption generation must be documented and reviewed.
6. Exceptions and unresolved issues must be recorded with an owner, due date and remediation plan.
7. Publication verification must confirm the real audience experience, not just file delivery.
8. The final asset must be preserved with provenance, metadata, version history and audit evidence.

## Summary
The production-management repository provides the operational governance and evidence model. The Closed Caption Subtitles repository provides the detailed editorial, technical and accessibility rules for captions.

Together, they form a coherent production ecosystem:
- production planning and accountability
- caption quality and delivery requirements
- release gate validation
- audience-facing access verification
- AI governance and human accountability
- end-to-end evidence retention

